WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.200
STEPHANIE WONG: Hey, everyone.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.410
This is Stephanie Wong,
and we are back today

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:14.230
with networking
specialist Ryan Przbyl.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:16.975
You might recognize him from
our popular series, "Networking

00:00:16.975 --> 00:00:20.410
101," "102," three,
four, and five, where

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:21.970
he covered Google
Cloud's networking

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:23.480
fundamentals with me.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.080
And last time, he discussed
how enterprise networking

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:29.590
in the cloud is changing from
traditional to service-oriented

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:32.920
networking, a mentality
shift for companies and cloud

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:34.090
providers alike.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:37.330
And today, we're covering how
you can approach these changes

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:39.370
and how Google
Cloud, specifically,

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.750
is addressing it and
evolving with it.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.470
So before I start, I just want
to mention, please, please

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:45.880
comment below with questions.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.890
Anything that we didn't
get to last time,

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.150
we are going to take
a look at all of them.

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:52.030
So definitely chat
us, and make sure

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:54.130
that we can get to
all of your questions.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:57.100
And for Ryan, to
kick it right off,

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:58.840
last time, we talked
about customers

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.970
moving newer
applications to the cloud

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:04.450
and adopting service-oriented
networking functionalities.

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:07.690
So what do we provide
as a stepping stone

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:11.440
to enable enterprises who do
use this traditional networking

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:12.670
model?

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:13.420
RYAN PRZBYL: Yeah.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:18.430
So I think Google is continuing
to evolve our networking layer

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:21.598
constructs, and I think all the
cloud providers are doing this.

00:01:21.598 --> 00:01:23.890
And there's a lot of interesting
internal conversations

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:26.500
that I have with engineers
and product managers.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.110
I think everybody wants to
work on the newest, latest,

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:30.450
greatest things.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.200
And I'm constantly
having to remind people

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.000
that there's a lot
of fundamental stuff

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.590
that we still need
to work on as well.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:38.080
And maybe it's not the bright,
shiny object, sexy thing

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.260
to work on, but it's very
critical for an enterprise.

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:45.430
The more products and tools
I can offer to replicate

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:48.280
your data center environment--
which I touched on last time--

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.480
the more of those
features I have,

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:55.600
the more you can pick and
choose how you want to migrate

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:57.700
and what stepping stones
you want to integrate.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.077
So you could have like, "OK,
I'm going to move like this,

00:02:00.077 --> 00:02:02.410
and it's going to be literally
a mirror image of my data

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:04.493
center--" and that assumes
we had every networking

00:02:04.493 --> 00:02:07.310
product working everywhere you
wanted it, you could do that--

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:10.442
and then say, "After I migrate
to 10,000 applications,

00:02:10.442 --> 00:02:12.400
I'm going to make this
change, and that's going

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.050
be my first stepping stone."

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.450
The reality is none
of the cloud providers

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:17.920
are completely there yet.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.190
We're having to literally
re-engineer 50 years of telecom

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:24.100
into this cloud
environment, into the SDN,

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.520
and we're building all sorts
of products and features

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.320
that maybe have been
very traditional

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:30.670
for many, many years.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.170
But the bottom line
is because we're

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:34.670
building all these constructs,
not necessarily from scratch,

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:36.640
but we have to build
all those features in.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.300
So as an example, if you do
a lot of routing with, say,

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:42.310
communities and
local pref and things

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:44.800
like that at a routing
layer, that's not something

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.750
you could just bring
into Google Cloud

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:48.550
today and use a Cloud
Router to do that.

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:51.760
So that's a point of
function that isn't there

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:53.560
that we need to
continue to evolve.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:56.440
And again, all cloud
providers are in this boat.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:59.320
So in terms of those
stepping stones,

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.470
I want to be able to continue
to push Google and continue

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.680
to build all of
those tools that give

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.600
the customer those
stepping stones to say,

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.790
"I want to make these
little incremental changes."

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:12.925
The worst thing, the
worst thing I have to do

00:03:12.925 --> 00:03:15.050
is get with the customer
and say, "You can't do it.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:17.720
It has to be done this way,"
because as I mentioned before,

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:20.170
depending on what that
particular way is,

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:23.560
that could be a very
big chunk to bite off

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:26.680
and be very difficult for
the customer to adopt.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.030
So I'm always cognizant
of that, and that's why--

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:31.030
again, coming with a very
traditional networking

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:32.560
background, I'm
always pushing us

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.810
to keep developing those
small stepping stones

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:38.620
that customers can place to
help them make that change.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.500
Now, a good example of like--

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:46.270
let's say you were a traditional
network infrastructure setup,

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:48.220
and you wanted to, say,
start introducing some

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:50.690
of these service constructs.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:53.440
So I talked about PSC, Private
Service Connect, before.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.290
You could say, "I want
to use PSC to front-end

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.270
a particular Google
API, and I just

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:00.110
want to try it out that way."

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:01.848
Or maybe you're using
a cloud service.

00:04:01.848 --> 00:04:03.640
Maybe you're using
something like Snowflake

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:07.180
or something else that's
really designed and grew up

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.200
in the cloud.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.630
And that would be a
great place to introduce

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:13.698
some of these service layer
constructs into the model,

00:04:13.698 --> 00:04:16.240
where you're not trying to all
of a sudden change everything,

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.615
but you're just saying, "I
want to front maybe the Google

00:04:18.615 --> 00:04:21.329
APIs with a PSC endpoint."

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:23.167
Tons of customers use
Google APIs every day

00:04:23.167 --> 00:04:24.250
to do all sorts of things.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.350
That would be a
great stepping stone.

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:29.253
And then you get to
understand how does PSC work.

00:04:29.253 --> 00:04:30.670
STEPHANIE WONG:
Just talking about

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:33.400
for the people who do want
visibility and control

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:35.860
over building something
like a NAT gateway

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:38.170
and handling
routing, what's been

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:40.630
the response from providers
like Google Cloud?

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:41.380
RYAN PRZBYL: Yeah.

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:43.960
So this goes back to
like, there's still things

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:44.990
that we are building.

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:48.160
So like when you take PSC
and it's being a managed

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:49.717
service, where
we're handling that

00:04:49.717 --> 00:04:51.550
and we're handling
routing under the covers,

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:53.740
let's take the NAT case.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:55.990
We understand that there's
still product development

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:58.060
work that we have to do
on that functionality,

00:04:58.060 --> 00:04:59.960
and we are constantly evolving.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:05.140
So this is what I'm referring
to when we talk about customers

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:07.852
aren't always comfortable
with losing that visibility,

00:05:07.852 --> 00:05:09.560
but it's incumbent on
your cloud provider

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:11.143
to understand where
you're coming from

00:05:11.143 --> 00:05:14.180
and why you're coming from
there and continue to evolve

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.010
their networking technology.

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:19.470
Now, that being said,
NATing is nothing new.

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:21.290
It is a very old
technology that's

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:22.680
been around for a long time.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.440
It's just certain
levels of functionality

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:26.090
we have yet to develop.

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:27.850
So I think this is a key point.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.100
We talked a little bit about--

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.820
I think most people agree
the service networking

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:33.410
model is the way forward.

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:36.020
If you look 20 years
from now, everybody

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.000
is going to be using
that paradigm, I think.

00:05:40.623 --> 00:05:43.040
And it's easy to continue to
say, "That's the cutting edge

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:43.540
stuff.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:46.100
Let's go and develop that
stuff," versus something,

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:49.340
say, like NATing that
is an old technology.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.830
One approach is, "Oh, let
the customers manage it.

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:53.180
They can put their own
NAT gateways in there,"

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:54.638
or "They can do
things themselves,"

00:05:54.638 --> 00:05:57.530
and there are ways
to do this stuff.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:05:59.690
But we want to continue
to enable customers

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:02.140
with easy-to-use,
easy-to-consume services.

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:04.910
So like I said, we're doing
more work on the NAT front

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:06.990
to expand out capabilities.

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:11.000
So a customer who is maybe not
entirely comfortable using PSC

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:13.880
because they lose some
visibility things, we're

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:16.160
giving them more and more
of that network layer

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.300
functionality so that they can
build, again, what they have

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:23.360
in the data center to replicate
it as much as possible to give

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:25.970
them that comfort factor of
getting into cloud and then,

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:28.045
once they're in
cloud, start to look

00:06:28.045 --> 00:06:29.420
at some these
other technologies.

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:30.337
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:06:30.337 --> 00:06:33.200
And I do know that we have some
visibility and intelligence

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:33.720
options.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:35.690
For example, we just came
out in the last couple

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:37.898
of years Network Intelligence
Center, which gives you

00:06:37.898 --> 00:06:40.130
visibility for
things like running

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:42.230
connectivity test
between endpoints

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:45.080
or monitoring your
performance through Dashboard

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:48.480
or in looking at your topology
from a visual standpoint.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:50.540
And so this seems like
it's more in the realm

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:53.240
of a service-oriented model,
but do you consider this

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:55.790
an overlapping area,
where we can start

00:06:55.790 --> 00:07:00.350
to build bridges and allow
traditional networking

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:02.660
architects and
engineers to understand

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:05.330
how to get visibility in a
service-oriented environment?

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:06.440
RYAN PRZBYL: Yeah.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:08.997
It's really interesting
because I've been in the space

00:07:08.997 --> 00:07:10.830
for 20 years, and I
have touched I don't how

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:14.120
many thousands of networks, but
they're all built differently.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:17.960
Nobody builds-- two networks
are like a fingerprint.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:19.760
They're all slightly
different, and it's

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:21.750
our job to understand that.

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:24.050
But one fundamental is being
able to troubleshoot them

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:25.580
when they break.

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:28.520
And as I mentioned earlier,
when you move into the cloud

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:30.980
and you move into
our SDN and how

00:07:30.980 --> 00:07:32.990
things have been
built under the hood,

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:37.400
that isn't always easily
understandable by customers.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:40.700
So tools like Network
Intelligence Center

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:43.652
are designed to help
bridge that gap.

00:07:43.652 --> 00:07:45.110
You may have a very
traditional way

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.210
of troubleshooting your
network and saying, "OK, I

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:48.770
can see all these paths."

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:50.730
But things like a
traceroute, for example,

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:54.520
which is a very common
troubleshooting tool--

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:56.270
it doesn't always give
you the information

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:58.490
that maybe it would in your data
center where you can see like,

00:07:58.490 --> 00:07:59.390
"OK, I'm hopping here.

00:07:59.390 --> 00:07:59.990
I'm hopping here.

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:00.890
I'm hopping into a firewall.

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:01.800
I'm going through the firewall.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:03.470
I'm hopping to another router."

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:06.510
You don't typically get
that kind of information.

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:10.040
So just as an example,
when you build a VM,

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:12.080
we program route
tables onto that VM.

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:14.180
We program firewall
rules on that VM.

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:17.210
So if traffic isn't
getting to that VM,

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:19.118
is it because it can't
route to that VM?

00:08:19.118 --> 00:08:20.660
Is it because there's
a firewall rule

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:22.040
that's blocking the traffic?

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:24.993
So again, from a traditional
networking standpoint of view,

00:08:24.993 --> 00:08:26.660
you might use traceroute
and say, "Oh, I

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:28.760
see it went from
the original server.

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:29.750
It went to the router.

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:31.458
From the router, it
went to the firewall.

00:08:31.458 --> 00:08:33.140
And then, I see it stop.

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:35.252
Oh, well, it's the firewall
that's blocking it."

00:08:35.252 --> 00:08:37.460
But what do you do when the
firewall, the routes, all

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:39.620
of this stuff live on the VM?

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:41.450
What is it that's
actually causing

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:43.440
the packet not to get there?

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:45.920
So that's where things like
Network Intelligence Center,

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.553
they help bridge that gap.

00:08:47.553 --> 00:08:48.470
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:50.095
And I think it's key
to note that there

00:08:50.095 --> 00:08:53.270
are tools and modes
of thinking that still

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:54.780
are applicable in this case.

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:57.650
You can always run a traceroute
even if your applications live

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:00.680
in the cloud, and we also have
tools in logging and monitoring

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:02.960
like Cloud Trace,
so I think it's just

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:05.510
understanding where to
map certain concepts

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:09.260
and how they translate
into the cloud.

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:11.180
But do you find most
companies, in general,

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:14.570
who operate in a
network-centric model are more

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:17.390
or less eager to adopt
this service-oriented model

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:19.553
of networking?

00:09:19.553 --> 00:09:20.720
RYAN PRZBYL: Eager to adopt?

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:24.020
Like I said, I think,
depending on who you talk to,

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:25.580
it might be a yes,
it might be a no.

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:27.470
Again, having a traditional
network engineering background,

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:29.678
it's a little bit scary when
you start talking about,

00:09:29.678 --> 00:09:32.390
"We're going to put a whole
bunch of sidecar envoy proxies

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:33.380
in there, and we're
going to start

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:35.510
talking about service meshes
and service discovery,

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:37.802
and we're going to start
abstracting the network, where

00:09:37.802 --> 00:09:38.900
we're really just--

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:40.220
this service needs to
connect to another service.

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:41.762
We'll just push it
to the Envoy Proxy

00:09:41.762 --> 00:09:45.140
and let the Envoy Proxy figure
out how to handle this."

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:48.770
So again, you're abstracting
the network away.

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:50.420
As a networking guy,
it's like, "Well,

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:52.158
are you abstracting
my job away?"

00:09:52.158 --> 00:09:54.200
This is what I've been
dealing with for 20 years.

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:55.533
And the answer is ultimately no.

00:09:55.533 --> 00:09:57.110
The network still exists.

00:09:57.110 --> 00:09:59.605
But it's a fundamental
shift, like a mental shift,

00:09:59.605 --> 00:10:01.730
that you're having to make
to adapt with the times.

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:05.570
So I think most people see
that is the path forward,

00:10:05.570 --> 00:10:08.925
but on one level,
it seems simple.

00:10:08.925 --> 00:10:10.800
You talk about like,
"Oh, we're going to talk

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:11.842
about service networking.

00:10:11.842 --> 00:10:13.758
We're talking about
service meshes and service

00:10:13.758 --> 00:10:14.340
discoveries."

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:17.940
And even if you decide to
break a monolithic app down

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:21.300
into microservice
and things like that,

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:23.920
it's easy to whiteboard
this stuff out sometimes.

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:26.310
Implementing it is a
total another ballgame.

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:29.130
If you want to see a
really good video on this,

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:31.020
if you go to the
Envoy Proxy website,

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:34.170
Matt Klein over at Lyft
did a history of Lyft

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:38.550
and how they integrated a
lot of these level constructs

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:41.080
into Lyft's environment.

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:42.463
And it is a great--

00:10:42.463 --> 00:10:43.880
trying to get your
hand or heads--

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:47.430
or getting your head around
a monolithic app being broken

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:49.380
to these smaller
services and really

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:52.815
what service networking
really looks like and all

00:10:52.815 --> 00:10:53.940
the complexities around it.

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:55.080
It's probably an
hour-long watch,

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:57.330
but I thought Matt did a
really good job of describing

00:10:57.330 --> 00:10:59.680
like, "Hey, this seems
easy on one hand,

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:02.800
but in reality, it is
a lot more complex."

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:06.240
So to your point, this
is why these very large,

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:07.890
traditional enterprises--

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:10.110
I think they see
that path forward,

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:12.780
but it's super scary when you
start watching videos like that

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:14.780
and you start dissecting
this and say, "I've got

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:15.960
to move 5,000 applications."

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:18.270
And if somebody wants
to use this model,

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:19.970
there's all this
complexity that's

00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:22.470
going to get added to things
and things I have to solve for.

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:23.887
And, oh, by the
way, this is a way

00:11:23.887 --> 00:11:26.290
of thinking that I maybe
never had to have in the past.

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:29.910
So I think a lot of
people see the vision,

00:11:29.910 --> 00:11:33.100
but again, implementing it is
totally different ball game.

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:35.160
And that's where it
becomes incumbent on having

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:38.610
all those networking constructs
to make it easy and path

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:40.500
of least resistance
to move into the cloud

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:41.850
for a large enterprise.

00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:44.820
And I call this a Grow
with Google Model.

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:46.410
So while we have a
lot of people that

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:50.910
are hyped on the service
architecture model,

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:53.220
it's like the Grand Canyon.

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:55.740
I can't expect somebody to
leap across the Grand Canyon.

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:57.510
I have to get them
into the environment,

00:11:57.510 --> 00:11:59.760
and then, I have to build
these small stepping stones.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:01.200
I have to start
introducing things

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:03.330
like PSC, like I
mentioned, like using it

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:05.490
to maybe front-end Google APIs.

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:07.170
Introduce it as a
small stepping stone

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:10.073
so that they can take
these small steps to get

00:12:10.073 --> 00:12:11.490
across the Grand
Canyon and aren't

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:13.733
trying to make this
fundamental leap from "Here's

00:12:13.733 --> 00:12:15.150
where I am today"
to "Here's where

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:16.982
Google sees us in the future."

00:12:16.982 --> 00:12:18.690
STEPHANIE WONG: But
I mean, you're right.

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:21.023
It's growing with Google, and
I think we're continuously

00:12:21.023 --> 00:12:23.490
investing in providing
those stepping stones that

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:26.250
better translate and map to
traditional networking models.

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:28.170
Meanwhile, customers
are also becoming

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:31.010
more accustomed to
adopting and taking

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:32.760
on the approach of
service-oriented models

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:35.520
with the emergence of
their newer applications

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:36.510
that are cloud-native.

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:37.155
RYAN PRZBYL: Yeah.

00:12:37.155 --> 00:12:38.738
In what I mentioned
in the first video

00:12:38.738 --> 00:12:40.710
too, there's the
technology aspect of it,

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:42.960
how to implement the technology,
but then there's the,

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:45.270
how does the
organization operate?

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:48.293
I can build a lot
of things in Google,

00:12:48.293 --> 00:12:50.460
and we build tons of different
network architectures

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:51.362
with our customers.

00:12:51.362 --> 00:12:53.070
But at some point, I
have to hand it over

00:12:53.070 --> 00:12:55.260
to an operations team,
and the only thing

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:57.600
I can ever guarantee about
any network that I build

00:12:57.600 --> 00:12:59.970
is it will break at some point.

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:02.250
That's just the one guarantee
I'm willing to make.

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:04.892
So it's not just a matter
of take the technology

00:13:04.892 --> 00:13:06.600
and implement technology,
but it's also--

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:08.767
like I was mentioning in
the previous conversation--

00:13:08.767 --> 00:13:11.160
thinking about how you
operationalize this stuff.

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:12.870
Like, what operational
impacts does it

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:15.780
have to your existing monitoring
suite, the existing tools

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:18.990
you use, the existing
processes, the existing mindset,

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:20.940
that a network
engineer goes through

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:23.080
to troubleshoot a problem?

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:25.335
If you're trying to abstract
more of the network away,

00:13:25.335 --> 00:13:26.710
you've got to
think about, "Well,

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:29.160
how do I troubleshoot problems
when the service doesn't work?"

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:30.827
If I don't have
visibility to everything

00:13:30.827 --> 00:13:33.630
and maybe I'm using some Google
tools that are new to me,

00:13:33.630 --> 00:13:36.565
it's going to be a little bit
of a cultural shift, right?

00:13:36.565 --> 00:13:37.440
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:39.210
And you were just
talking about having

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:41.820
to overcome technical
debt, and we're

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:44.010
trying to provide a
path of least resistance

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:45.070
for our customers.

00:13:45.070 --> 00:13:46.920
But what does that
begin to look like?

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:48.750
What is Google Cloud
doing in this space

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.315
to provide an easier path
or a lower barrier to entry?

00:13:51.315 --> 00:13:53.190
RYAN PRZBYL: So again,
I think this goes back

00:13:53.190 --> 00:13:55.170
to having the right
tools in the toolbox.

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:58.770
And to me, tools are
features and capabilities

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:00.390
at a networking layer.

00:14:00.390 --> 00:14:03.923
So the path of least
resistance, ideally, is,

00:14:03.923 --> 00:14:05.340
"I'm going to take
my data center,

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:07.950
and I'm going to forklift it
in the entire same way that

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:10.813
operates today in Google
Cloud with zero changes."

00:14:10.813 --> 00:14:13.230
That would be the ideal, I
guess, path of least resistance

00:14:13.230 --> 00:14:15.990
because literally
nothing changes.

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:18.820
The bottom line is that's
basically impossible.

00:14:18.820 --> 00:14:21.750
You can't do it for
a number of reasons.

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:24.878
So when we're having those
conversations with customers,

00:14:24.878 --> 00:14:26.670
we talk about, what
are the stepping stones

00:14:26.670 --> 00:14:30.090
that a particular
customer can adopt

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:33.240
and that they can get
their head around using?

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:36.317
Even just the concept
of our basic-level VPC,

00:14:36.317 --> 00:14:37.900
if you're talking
about a data center,

00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:39.400
a data center is a
physical building

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:42.038
and a physical location.

00:14:42.038 --> 00:14:44.580
And your network, when you start
to talk about the local area

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:47.700
network or in that data center,
maybe it's in Dallas, Texas,

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:48.433
as an example.

00:14:48.433 --> 00:14:50.850
And then you move into cloud,
and you're like, "Well, now,

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:52.620
I have this construct
of a VPC, which

00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:54.780
is like my data
center network, but it

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:57.190
can be a global construct.

00:14:57.190 --> 00:14:58.175
OK, that's interesting.

00:14:58.175 --> 00:14:59.800
Well, how do I set
up routing in there?

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:00.420
How do I do all this stuff?

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:01.170
Oh."

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:02.753
We tell people all
the time like, "You

00:15:02.753 --> 00:15:05.070
don't have to do a lot
because our Andromeda SDN will

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:06.505
take care of a lot of things."

00:15:06.505 --> 00:15:08.380
It's different from a
lot of our competitors,

00:15:08.380 --> 00:15:11.200
where you're having to build a
lot of network infrastructure,

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:13.840
like VPNs or other things to
connect your region A to region

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:16.210
B. That starts to
look, in some ways,

00:15:16.210 --> 00:15:17.742
like connecting
two data centers.

00:15:17.742 --> 00:15:19.450
I've got to build a
network between them.

00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:20.560
Then, you come to
Google, and it's like,

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.780
"Well, I can run stuff in Europe
and I can run stuff in APAC,

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:24.590
and they can seamlessly
talk together?"

00:15:24.590 --> 00:15:25.090
Yeah.

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:26.860
"And you don't have
to build any network?"

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:28.550
Sure, we take care
of all that for you.

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:32.770
Again, we've abstracted
it under the hood.

00:15:32.770 --> 00:15:35.560
So that's just an example of--

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:38.680
that, in itself, is a
fundamental difference.

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:41.875
You could build a VPC that
exists only in one region

00:15:41.875 --> 00:15:43.750
and make it look and
feel like a data center,

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:45.792
but generally speaking,
I would advise a customer

00:15:45.792 --> 00:15:46.430
not to do that.

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:48.443
So that would be one of
those stepping stones.

00:15:48.443 --> 00:15:50.110
"Let's start thinking
about your network

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:53.050
as this global
construct that exists

00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:55.660
in a different way than an
individual data center does."

00:15:55.660 --> 00:15:59.373
So the more tools I
have in the toolbox--

00:15:59.373 --> 00:16:00.790
and again, when I
say "tools," I'm

00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:03.490
talking about product
features to say, OK,

00:16:03.490 --> 00:16:06.790
you do things this way
today, and changing

00:16:06.790 --> 00:16:10.130
that piece of your environment
is too difficult for you to do?

00:16:10.130 --> 00:16:10.630
OK.

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:12.070
I want to be able
to let you move

00:16:12.070 --> 00:16:13.373
that over apples to apples.

00:16:13.373 --> 00:16:15.040
But this other piece
of the environment,

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:16.810
maybe we can move from
apples to oranges.

00:16:16.810 --> 00:16:19.000
And this other piece
I can more drastically

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:21.370
change from apples to cherries.

00:16:21.370 --> 00:16:23.520
That's what I do every day.

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:25.270
I am talking to customers
and figuring out

00:16:25.270 --> 00:16:27.850
how do we move from here
to here but get some

00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:30.130
of the advantages of cloud?

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:32.693
Even if we're talking about
just network-centric things.

00:16:32.693 --> 00:16:35.110
We're not talking about any
of the service-oriented model.

00:16:35.110 --> 00:16:36.400
Let's talk about what
your network looks

00:16:36.400 --> 00:16:37.150
like two years out.

00:16:37.150 --> 00:16:38.525
What do you want
it to look like,

00:16:38.525 --> 00:16:40.900
and how do we help
you take it from where

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:43.750
it is today to where you want
it to be in two years, three

00:16:43.750 --> 00:16:45.205
years, four years?

00:16:45.205 --> 00:16:46.080
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:47.610
And I think that
eases the concern

00:16:47.610 --> 00:16:50.100
when you are looking at it
from the perspective of being

00:16:50.100 --> 00:16:53.010
a partner over the span
of years, as opposed to,

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:56.100
we're asking you to make
an entire shift of model

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:59.800
and thinking in the span
of six months, but rather,

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:03.200
how can we get you to this new
world of service orientation

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:05.700
over the span of two to three
years, which is more, I think,

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:08.640
reasonable for people to accept.

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:10.398
And then I think
you've also mentioned

00:17:10.398 --> 00:17:11.940
that you have a
viewpoint that Google

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:13.950
Cloud and other providers
should be providing

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:16.680
more traditional networking
tools or at least the ability

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:18.270
to translate what
you've been doing

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:20.550
in your current
environment into the cloud.

00:17:20.550 --> 00:17:22.380
So when you say
these things, do you

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:24.930
mean offer more
customization, the ability

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:27.660
to control more of their routing
layers, things like that,

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:30.990
or is it more just about
tools in the toolbox?

00:17:30.990 --> 00:17:31.740
RYAN PRZBYL: Yeah.

00:17:31.740 --> 00:17:33.157
I mean, again,
when I say "tools,"

00:17:33.157 --> 00:17:35.490
I'm talking about product
features and things like that.

00:17:35.490 --> 00:17:37.650
So the one I mentioned
earlier with routing is,

00:17:37.650 --> 00:17:41.320
let's say you do a whole bunch
with BGP-filtering policies

00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:43.320
and things like that, or
you're using local pref

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:45.973
in a certain way
to drive routing.

00:17:45.973 --> 00:17:48.390
That's not something we offer
today with our Cloud Router.

00:17:48.390 --> 00:17:50.220
It's something I'm talking to
the Cloud Router team about

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:51.637
and saying like,
"Hey, what can we

00:17:51.637 --> 00:17:55.770
do to offer more and more of
these basic-level features

00:17:55.770 --> 00:17:57.840
that customers are using
within their data center

00:17:57.840 --> 00:17:59.760
to manipulate routing?"

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:02.790
So those are the type
of things that I'm

00:18:02.790 --> 00:18:04.110
talking about with the teams.

00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:08.880
And when I say, "Offer these
tools or these products,"

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:10.630
the more of those I
have in the toolbox--

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:12.858
and I use that term
loosely, a "toolbox"--

00:18:12.858 --> 00:18:15.150
it allows us, again, to create
that apple-to-apple sort

00:18:15.150 --> 00:18:15.795
of scenario.

00:18:15.795 --> 00:18:17.670
Now, the one thing that's
different that I do

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:22.860
want to talk about too is,
when you build a data center,

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:24.810
it's typically a very
capital-intensive thing.

00:18:24.810 --> 00:18:25.410
You're going out
there, and you're

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:27.195
buying all this
equipment, and it's

00:18:27.195 --> 00:18:29.348
very-- it's what we
call heavy CapEx model.

00:18:29.348 --> 00:18:31.140
You can buy all these
routers and switches,

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:32.765
and they have a whole
list of features.

00:18:32.765 --> 00:18:34.620
And you put them
in the data center,

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:38.730
and you use them for five years,
10 years, 15 years, whatever.

00:18:38.730 --> 00:18:41.790
And then what happens is,
once they've lived their life,

00:18:41.790 --> 00:18:43.482
you have another
big capital spend,

00:18:43.482 --> 00:18:44.940
and you upgrade
that router and you

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:47.315
get a whole much more of
feature functionality out of it,

00:18:47.315 --> 00:18:48.570
potentially.

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:51.395
This is the mindset
that people have done.

00:18:51.395 --> 00:18:52.770
"I built the data
center day one,

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:54.510
and it's going to live
that way for the next five

00:18:54.510 --> 00:18:55.380
years or 10 years.

00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:56.820
I'm going to make small
incremental changes,

00:18:56.820 --> 00:18:59.490
but I'm not going to make any
fundamental changes because I

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:02.130
spent all this
capital over here."

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:03.810
Cloud flips that
model on its head.

00:19:03.810 --> 00:19:05.320
You're not buying
routers anymore.

00:19:05.320 --> 00:19:07.842
It is a very OpEx-driven model.

00:19:07.842 --> 00:19:10.050
So you're paying for these
services month over month.

00:19:10.050 --> 00:19:11.910
You're not saying, "I have
this huge bucket of cash

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:12.670
I'm spending here.

00:19:12.670 --> 00:19:13.690
And then 10 years
down the road, I'm

00:19:13.690 --> 00:19:15.910
going to have to get another
huge bucket of cash."

00:19:15.910 --> 00:19:18.310
So why is that important?

00:19:18.310 --> 00:19:19.978
Well, in this OpEx
model, where you're

00:19:19.978 --> 00:19:22.020
buying more managed
services-- like I was saying,

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:25.440
we're constantly introducing
new features and new tools

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:27.360
in the toolbox to
change the architecture

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:29.590
and to change the way
that you can do things.

00:19:29.590 --> 00:19:31.882
So if you have this mentality
of, "I build it this way,

00:19:31.882 --> 00:19:33.715
and then, it's going
to live for five years,

00:19:33.715 --> 00:19:35.310
and I'm going to
change it," you're

00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:37.890
not getting the full
advantage out of cloud.

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:39.960
The whole point of
cloud, in some ways,

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:43.230
is to be able to make
changes more periodically.

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:46.020
Even if-- again, go back to
the customer who yelled at me

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:48.120
and said, "It's very
difficult for us to do."

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:49.890
The more you can
adopt that mindset,

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:52.230
the more you can
capitalize on the evolution

00:19:52.230 --> 00:19:53.310
that cloud is making.

00:19:53.310 --> 00:19:55.620
We're launching new
features, and we're

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:56.910
giving you new capabilities.

00:19:56.910 --> 00:19:58.560
You want to continuously
be integrating them,

00:19:58.560 --> 00:20:01.060
not thinking like you do in a
traditional data center model,

00:20:01.060 --> 00:20:02.107
where, "I got this model.

00:20:02.107 --> 00:20:03.690
I'm going to change
it in five years."

00:20:03.690 --> 00:20:05.933
It's like, I tell
customers every six months,

00:20:05.933 --> 00:20:07.350
"You should be
looking at, what do

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:08.680
we need to change
in the architecture?

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:10.430
How do we roll this
architecture forward?"

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:13.230
But if you're taking those
incremental changes every six

00:20:13.230 --> 00:20:16.960
months, it's much easier to
get to your two-year vision,

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:19.770
your five-year
vision versus maybe

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:21.090
that traditional mentality.

00:20:21.090 --> 00:20:23.673
So again, I think people need
to think about cloud differently

00:20:23.673 --> 00:20:25.020
just from that standpoint.

00:20:25.020 --> 00:20:26.010
It's an OpEx model.

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:28.380
You're not spending CapEx,
which should make it easier

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:32.460
for you to be integrating these
changes over the course of time

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:34.830
versus making these
very large step function

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:37.290
changes to the way things
are running in the cloud

00:20:37.290 --> 00:20:37.945
environment.

00:20:37.945 --> 00:20:38.820
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:20:38.820 --> 00:20:40.680
I think you having
that background

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:42.600
in traditional
networking helps you

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:45.280
understand where a lot of these
customers are coming from.

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:48.220
And it is about
crawl, walk, and run.

00:20:48.220 --> 00:20:50.100
But I do see that some
customers are probably

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:52.158
going to think, "OK,
well, I don't really

00:20:52.158 --> 00:20:54.450
have a project that would
require me to be in the cloud

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:56.640
just yet because everything's
operating on premise."

00:20:56.640 --> 00:20:59.550
But perhaps there is a team
that is the cloud disruptor

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:02.642
at the company, who might have
a test project or a test dev

00:21:02.642 --> 00:21:05.100
environment that they want to
start operating in the cloud.

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:08.730
And so, thus, I think that
relationship between network

00:21:08.730 --> 00:21:10.650
and the developers
or other teams

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:14.100
would be important in that case
to actually identify these use

00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:16.110
cases, where you
can start adopting

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:18.040
these models in the cloud.

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:18.540
But I do--

00:21:18.540 --> 00:21:18.660
RYAN PRZBYL: Yup.

00:21:18.660 --> 00:21:20.827
STEPHANIE WONG: --want to
zoom out just a little bit

00:21:20.827 --> 00:21:23.550
and talk about the future of
where this is all heading.

00:21:23.550 --> 00:21:25.800
Where do you see cloud
networking heading?

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:27.960
I see that we're
getting to a point

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:29.833
where things are
converging with networking

00:21:29.833 --> 00:21:31.000
and service-oriented models.

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:33.750
But what do you think is going
to be the future for cloud

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:36.000
and for Google
Cloud in this space?

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:37.980
RYAN PRZBYL: So
it's definitely--

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:40.950
as we've been orbiting around
this service networking model,

00:21:40.950 --> 00:21:43.620
that is the future, hands down.

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:47.710
I don't see it being
anything other than this.

00:21:47.710 --> 00:21:51.530
And again, just look
at how Google operates.

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:54.500
We operate services even though,
like I talked about earlier,

00:21:54.500 --> 00:21:56.030
we run a very complex network.

00:21:56.030 --> 00:21:57.440
At the end of the day,
we don't run a network

00:21:57.440 --> 00:21:58.232
to run the network.

00:21:58.232 --> 00:21:59.090
We run services.

00:21:59.090 --> 00:22:01.100
The network is just part of it.

00:22:01.100 --> 00:22:03.020
And you look at--
so as an example,

00:22:03.020 --> 00:22:04.790
you look at how our
SRE teams operate.

00:22:04.790 --> 00:22:08.120
We have SRE teams that
operate in these verticals.

00:22:08.120 --> 00:22:09.650
So they operate this service.

00:22:09.650 --> 00:22:11.840
This service has
networking as part of it,

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:13.430
but there's a lot
of other components,

00:22:13.430 --> 00:22:15.830
and that's how our
SRE teams operate.

00:22:15.830 --> 00:22:19.220
Now, we have a networking SRE
team that goes horizontally

00:22:19.220 --> 00:22:21.490
across things, and
you're always going

00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:23.480
to have to have some
level of expertise there.

00:22:23.480 --> 00:22:26.990
But I see us as an
operating company

00:22:26.990 --> 00:22:28.700
who are operating services.

00:22:28.700 --> 00:22:31.220
We're building a structure
around these services

00:22:31.220 --> 00:22:31.820
themselves.

00:22:31.820 --> 00:22:34.070
And then, there's the stuff
that's wrapped around them

00:22:34.070 --> 00:22:36.260
to support those
services, and that

00:22:36.260 --> 00:22:38.690
includes networking and
all of the constructs

00:22:38.690 --> 00:22:40.340
that exist in networking.

00:22:40.340 --> 00:22:42.470
It will continue to
evolve to be more

00:22:42.470 --> 00:22:44.450
of this service-oriented
construct

00:22:44.450 --> 00:22:46.848
whether we like it or not.

00:22:46.848 --> 00:22:48.890
And like you said, I can
empathize with customers

00:22:48.890 --> 00:22:52.140
because there's some
level of uncomfortability,

00:22:52.140 --> 00:22:55.160
if that's a word, but I get
very uncomfortable just trying

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:57.980
to learn all that stuff and
trying to get my head around it

00:22:57.980 --> 00:22:59.900
because it's not my background.

00:22:59.900 --> 00:23:02.570
I'm the traditional router guy
like, "Let's build the routes.

00:23:02.570 --> 00:23:03.790
Let's do all this stuff.

00:23:03.790 --> 00:23:05.915
Let's not--" the whole
concept of service discovery

00:23:05.915 --> 00:23:09.205
and service meshes, I
get it at the surface.

00:23:09.205 --> 00:23:10.580
But when you peel
back the onion,

00:23:10.580 --> 00:23:12.038
you realize just
how complex it is.

00:23:12.038 --> 00:23:15.350
And when you don't have 20
years of experience doing it,

00:23:15.350 --> 00:23:16.790
it's very daunting.

00:23:16.790 --> 00:23:19.520
So I think, to answer
your direct question, that

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:20.420
is the future.

00:23:20.420 --> 00:23:22.670
20 years from now,
I think networking--

00:23:22.670 --> 00:23:24.740
there'll be a lot more
managed services in play

00:23:24.740 --> 00:23:27.920
that is obfuscating or managing
a lot of the networking.

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:30.470
And the network engineer
20 years from now

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:33.710
will probably be very different
than the network engineer who

00:23:33.710 --> 00:23:35.460
works in the industry today.

00:23:35.460 --> 00:23:37.010
So if you're a young
network engineer

00:23:37.010 --> 00:23:38.782
and you're just
getting started, you

00:23:38.782 --> 00:23:40.490
want to start digesting
all of that stuff

00:23:40.490 --> 00:23:42.860
because that's going to be
very commonplace in 10 years.

00:23:42.860 --> 00:23:44.693
Hopefully, at that
point, maybe I'm retired,

00:23:44.693 --> 00:23:46.320
and I don't have
to worry about it.

00:23:46.320 --> 00:23:49.040
But that is where
things are going.

00:23:49.040 --> 00:23:52.115
This is the whole point of
having these conversations.

00:23:52.115 --> 00:23:54.710
Three years ago, I wasn't
having these conversations.

00:23:54.710 --> 00:23:55.640
Now--

00:23:55.640 --> 00:23:55.970
STEPHANIE WONG: Yes.

00:23:55.970 --> 00:23:56.990
RYAN PRZBYL: --more and
more of the conversations

00:23:56.990 --> 00:23:59.165
are like this
service-oriented construct

00:23:59.165 --> 00:24:00.540
and what is Google
offering here.

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:02.310
What is Google's
vision of the future?

00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:04.852
And it really is
all of these things.

00:24:04.852 --> 00:24:07.310
Is traditional networking ever
going to completely go away?

00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:08.570
Absolutely not.

00:24:08.570 --> 00:24:10.220
It's always going to be there.

00:24:10.220 --> 00:24:12.590
Routers still exist,
and routes still

00:24:12.590 --> 00:24:15.500
exist to move bits and
bytes back and forth.

00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:17.450
It's just a matter
of how they're built

00:24:17.450 --> 00:24:19.700
and the constructs that we're
using over the top that,

00:24:19.700 --> 00:24:20.660
I think, change.

00:24:20.660 --> 00:24:23.403
And you start to be less focused
on the network and start--

00:24:23.403 --> 00:24:24.320
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:26.887
RYAN PRZBYL: --focusing more
on the services themselves.

00:24:26.887 --> 00:24:27.970
STEPHANIE WONG: All right.

00:24:27.970 --> 00:24:29.570
Well, thank you so
much again, Ryan,

00:24:29.570 --> 00:24:33.080
for covering this somewhat
less talked about topic.

00:24:33.080 --> 00:24:37.100
You have an important job in the
coming years to help customers

00:24:37.100 --> 00:24:39.230
move to and adopt this
service-oriented model

00:24:39.230 --> 00:24:40.070
in the cloud--

00:24:40.070 --> 00:24:41.630
at least before you're retired--

00:24:41.630 --> 00:24:43.340
and at the same
time, you want to be

00:24:43.340 --> 00:24:46.040
able to support more of a
traditional network-oriented

00:24:46.040 --> 00:24:48.020
model, whether
it's lift and shift

00:24:48.020 --> 00:24:50.420
or conversion into the cloud.

00:24:50.420 --> 00:24:53.010
So I just want to thank you
again for being on the show,

00:24:53.010 --> 00:24:55.910
and thank everybody else for
joining and for tuning in.

00:24:55.910 --> 00:24:56.660
RYAN PRZBYL: Yeah.

00:24:56.660 --> 00:24:59.040
And I'd love-- for people
that are watching this,

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:01.400
was this interesting to
hear for us to talk about?

00:25:01.400 --> 00:25:04.190
What are some other topics
you'd like to see us talk about?

00:25:04.190 --> 00:25:06.290
I have things I think are
great and interesting,

00:25:06.290 --> 00:25:07.850
but I'd love to hear
from people that

00:25:07.850 --> 00:25:10.040
are out there in the world.

00:25:10.040 --> 00:25:11.685
And I'm happy to
look at your comments

00:25:11.685 --> 00:25:13.310
and brainstorm about
how we can deliver

00:25:13.310 --> 00:25:14.700
some more content to you.

00:25:14.700 --> 00:25:15.950
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, exactly.

00:25:15.950 --> 00:25:18.020
So if there's anything
we covered today

00:25:18.020 --> 00:25:20.330
that was of interest or we
didn't get to a question

00:25:20.330 --> 00:25:23.330
that you might have about this
topic, please comment below.

00:25:23.330 --> 00:25:25.190
We are actually going
to read all of them

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:26.902
and respond to all
of your comments.

00:25:26.902 --> 00:25:28.610
Thank you so much,
everyone, for joining,

00:25:28.610 --> 00:25:31.500
and check out our next
episodes coming out.

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:32.497
Thanks for tuning in.

00:25:32.497 --> 00:25:33.330
RYAN PRZBYL: Thanks.

